<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Netherlands Trading Society</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> Trading Society (Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij) was a trading and financial company founded in 1824 on the initiative of King William I to revive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> commerce. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a> it held the monopoly on shipping and selling in the Netherlands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> that the government collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. It later became a major colonial bank and merged into ABN in 1964, a forerunner of ABN AMRO.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/netherlands-trading-society.jpg" alt="Netherlands Trading Society" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jane023 09:51, 20 February 2011 (UTC) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Bazel_Amsterdam_NHM_Nederlandsche_Handel-Maatschappij.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij, or NHM, was established by royal decree in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1824 to promote trade, shipping and agriculture, above all between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. It was a private company with publicly traded shares. Its founding was an attempt to bring new life to that trade after the depression of the years of French domination, 1795-1814, and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> two decades earlier, so that it is sometimes called the VOC's successor. With a playful nod to the older company's greatness it was known as Kompenie Ketjil, the little company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1826 the NHM opened an office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, known as De Factorij. Financing trade and shipping led it into banking: it moved overseas into banking in the 1870s and began banking in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1902. Branches followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1858, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> in 1888, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in 1906, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1920, these chiefly for the diamond trade. Its branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a>, opened in 1926 to serve Indonesian Muslims making the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, was the first commercial bank in the kingdom and until 1948 the only one.</p><p>In 1934 a financial restructuring cut its capital by 75 percent. It opened an agency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1941, became in 1948 the first foreign bank licensed by the new government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and in 1951 and 1954 opened branches in Mombasa, Dar-es-Salaam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. The Indonesian government nationalised its plantations in 1959 and its banking operations in 1960, forming Bank Ekspor Impor Indonesia, later Bank Mandiri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1964 the NHM merged with De Twentsche Bank to form Algemene Bank Nederland, a predecessor of ABN AMRO. Its former headquarters at Vijzelstraat 32 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, built in 1919-1926 and named De Bazel after its architect Karel de Bazel, is decorated with details recalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and its brickwork earned it the nickname spekkoek. The building now houses the Amsterdam Archives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Javanese villages were required to devote part of their land or labour to export crops such as coffee, sugar and indigo, delivered to the government at fixed prices and shipped by the <b>Netherlands Trading Society</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
